description:
My function `my_perform()`  uses `httr2::req_perform_iterative()` which has a `max_reqs` argument (default to 20). I'd like to test that `my_perform()` performs an infinite or very large number of iterative requests.

For now I am testing the argument:

```
test_that("my_perform() performs Inf requests by default", {
  expect_equal(formals(my_perform)$max_reqs, Inf)
})
```

Is this ideal or is it instead better to mock a large request? I created a fake response with data removed which allows for

```
with_mock_api({
  test_that("my_perform() returns API page_count responses", {
    responses<- my_req("my_endpoint") |> my_perform()
    expect_length(responses, 21)
  })
})
```

I also don't understand why the above requires 2 mock files,  `endpoint.R` and `endpoint-6fdf2d.R`.

I frequently use such a helper when working with REST APIs:

* to insert/update/delete many objects
* to get the information on many objects where the endpoint actually uses `POST` as it uses a body

Usually, there is a limit on the number of elements that can be updated/requested, so the requests have to be chunked.
This is kind of similar to `req_paginate()` but the user has to do the "pagination" himself.

Below is an example of an implementation I use:

```r
api_perform_in_chunks <- function(req,
                                  x,
                                  f_body,
                                  chunk_size,
                                  progress = NULL,
                                  error_call = caller_env()) {
  chunks <- vec_chop_by_size(x, chunk_size)
  with_indexed_errors(
    purrr::map(
      chunks,
      \(chunk) {
        req <- httr2::req_body_raw(
          req,
          jsonlite::toJSON(f_body(chunk))
        )
        api_perform(req)
      },
      .progress = progress
    ),
    message = "In chunk {.field {cnd$location}}.",
    error_call = error_call
  )
}

vec_chop_by_size <- function(x, size) {
  n <- vctrs::vec_size(x)
  sizes <- vctrs::vec_rep(size, n %/% size)
  if (n %% size != 0) {
    sizes <- c(sizes, n %% size)
  }
  sizes <- vctrs::vec_cast(sizes, integer())

  vec_partition(x, sizes)
}

vec_partition <- function(x, sizes) {
  size <- vctrs::vec_size(x)

  if (size != sum(sizes)) {
    abort("`sizes` must add up to size of `x`.")
  }

  starts <- c(1L, sizes)
  starts <- cumsum(starts)[-length(starts)]
  starts <- starts - 1L # 0-based

  vctrs:::vec_chop_seq(x, starts, sizes)
}
```

This would mean changing the arguments so that it takes a request as the first argument, and maybe automatically attaching the `parsed` data and `next_request` call back to that request.

That possibly implies that registering a parser is a more general feature, which we could possibly implement lazily. i.e. you'd register a parser in the request (with `req_parser()`?), and then call `resp_parsed()` (or maybe `resp_body_parsed()`) to access the parsed body. We'd need to add an environment to the response so that we could do the parsing once and cache it. And then _maybe_ have some helper that does `resp_parse(req_perform(req))`? And something similar for `req_perform_parallel()`?  That would make `req_perform_iterate()` fit in better.

@mgirlich these are very quick and rough notes, but does this make sense to you?

An example for `paginate_next_request()` would help in attempting to use

> a combination of req_perform() and paginate_next_request() to iterate through the pages yourself.

After digging in the test-paginate.R, I see this is the basic idea of `paginate_next_request()`:

```
req <- my_req() |> 
  httr2::req_paginate(
    next_request = function(req, parsed) {
      if(parsed$next_page == "") return(NULL)
      req |> req_url(parsed$next_page)
    }
  )

parsed <- req_perform(req) |> my_parse()
next_req <- req |> httr2::paginate_next_request(parsed = parsed)
```

Perhaps it is appropriate to include a full example of how one might perform the iteration, as per @hadley in https://github.com/r-lib/httr2/issues/8#issuecomment-866221516_:

```
`%||%` <- function(x, y) {
  if (is.null(x)) y else x
}

my_req_perform_paginate <- function(req, max_pages = NULL) {
  out <- vector("list", max_pages %||% 10)
  i <- 1L
  
  repeat({
    out[[i]] <- req_perform(req) |> mm_parse()
    if (!is.null(max_pages) && i == max_pages) {
      break
    }
    
    req <- req |> httr2::paginate_next_request(parsed = out[[i]])
    
    if (is.null(req)) {
      break
    }
    
    i <- i + 1L
    if (i > length(out)) {
      length(out) <- length(out) * 2L
    }
  })
  
  if (i != length(out)) {
    out <- out[seq_len(i)]
  }
  out
  
}
```

Currently `resp_abort()` is non-exported. It's nice to be able to use a consistent {httr2} codepath to possibly create new conditions, however. For example, we may want to signal on a 3xx error. Or, alternatively, we may want to capture a 4xx error, inspect the error reason, _alter the condition object_ and re-throw.

An example of that second use case is with some AWS APIs, where all 4xx errors are rolled into a single `400` response with the actual error reasons sent along in the response body. The [AWS Cognito Token endpoint](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/token-endpoint.html) is one such example. That endpoint can _also_ send 5xx errors back (which we'd like to signal as an error as usual), but we may take differential action depending on whether or not the underlying `400` error reason is "invalid_client". We might want to add an `invalid_client_error` class to the {httr2} 'standard' condition object, e.g., for easier handling by a function-caller upstream.

Detecting that situation might look like:
```r
tryCatch({
  resp <- req |> req_perform()
  ## handle non-error normally
}, httr2_http_400 = function(e) {
  reason <- attr(e, "resp") |> resp_body_string() |> parse_error_reason()
  if (identical(reason, "invalid_client")) {
    stop(structure(e, class = c("invalid_client_error", class(e))))  ## or perhaps rlang::cnd_signal()
  } else {
    stop(e)  ## re-throw; or perhaps rlang::cnd_signal()
  }
})
```
... which has that somewhat ugly `attr(e, "resp")` portion.

Another approach might be:
```r
resp <- req |> req_error(\(x) FALSE) |> req_perform()
if (resp_status(resp) == 400L) {
  reason <- resp |> resp_body_string() |> parse_error_reason()
  if (identical(reason, "invalid_client")) {
    ## now I want to 're-throw' what _would_ be the normal httr2 condition, but with a class added.
    ## instead, I need to do something like:
    cnd <- tryCatch(httr2:::resp_abort(resp), error = identity)  ## purposefully throw-and-catch just to get the condition :-|
    stop(structure(cnd, class = c("invalid_client_error", class(cnd))))
  } else {
    httr2:::resp_abort(resp)
  }
} else if (resp_status(resp) > 400L) {
  httr2:::resp_abort(resp)
} else {
  ## handle non-error normally
}
```
Both of these feel kinda clunky and I'd rather just be able to promote _any_ {httr2} response I want into a condition using something similar to:
```r
resp_cnd <- function(resp, type = c("error", "warning", "message"), cls = character(0), info = NULL) {
  ## builds a condition similarly to the current httr2::resp_abort,
  ## but who's class is `c(cls, "httr2_http_<status>", "httr2_http", "rlang_<type>", "<type>", "condition")`
}
```

Another advantage to such a condition-builder is the ability to more easily construct conditions for, say, 3xx-responses, on which we may want to raise warnings. (Unexpected redirects warrant warnings :-) Again, this is detectable and doable without exporting {httr2} condition-building, but the workarounds feel ... ugly(?), and there's some niceness to having a single consistent mechanism in place to create {httr2} condition objects that always have, e.g., the "resp" attribute set, along with the canonical `httr2_http_<status_code>` class.

Again, it goes without saying (though I'll say it anyway), this is only a polite thought-experiment veiling as a feature request, and if this is outside the scope of {httr2} I totally get it ... thanks again for the work and consideration in either outcome!

["User-supplied key" section](https://httr2.r-lib.org/articles/wrapping-apis.html#user-supplied-key-1) of the vignette says

> A good place to start is an environment variable, because environment variables are easy to set without typing anything in the console (which can get accidentally shared via your `.Rhistory`)

While I generally agree with the idea, I believe a package should also provide some measure to input secrets interactively, for example askpass package. I'd argue encouraging the use of an environmental variable cannot stop the users who want to try the functionality quickly; if the envvar is the only way to do so, they can easily type `Sys.setenv(YOURPACKAGE_KEY = "xxx")` in the console directly, which is not the consequence we wanted. So, how about adding these lines?

``` diff
  if (is_testing()) {
    testing_key()
+ } else if(interactive()) {
+   key <- askpass::askpass("NYTIMES_KEY")
  } else {
    stop("No API key found, please supply with `api_key` argument or with NYTIMES_KEY env var")  
  }
```

(Further, I prefer not to expose such an argument like `api_key`, but it's probably rather my personal preference.)

```R
resp_check_status <- function(resp, info = NULL) {
  check_response(resp)
  if (!resp_is_error(resp)) {
    return(invisible(resp))
  }

  abort(
    body = function(...) c(resp_auth_message(resp), info),
    status = resp_status(resp),
    resp = resp_status_desc(resp),
    class = c(glue("httr2_http_{status}"), "httr2_http")
  )
}

#' @export
cnd_header.httr2_http <- function(x) {
  glue_data(x, "HTTP {status} {desc}.")
}
#' @export
cnd_body.httr2_http <- function(x) {
  x$body
}
```
